notify-senechal.sh b60bac7 fixed the ahead-ness false FAIL (thank you) and immediately produced a SECOND false FAIL on the very next successful delivery, this time from the new landed() check: "scheduler -i reported success but the note is not in .claude/FOCUS.md on senechal remote". The note HAD landed -- verified independently: senechal origin/main .claude/FOCUS.md line 35, the full text intact and unwrapped, commit 633d3c2. Likely cause: in the ahead==0 branch, landed() runs `git show "@{u}:$focus_rel"` with NO `git fetch` first, so it reads whatever the local remote-tracking ref happened to be at that moment. The push it is verifying was performed by a DIFFERENT process (scheduler -i, via cmd_commit_file), and senechal has several concurrent writers, so @{u} is not reliably current at that instant -- the ahead>0 path does fetch before its equivalent check, the ahead==0 path does not. Suggested fix: `git fetch -q origin` immediately before computing ahead/behind, so both branches verify against a freshly-read remote rather than a cached ref. Worth also making the probe tolerant of the bullet prefix/wrapping cmd_idea applies, or probing for a short unique token instead of `head -c 60` of the raw text. Pattern worth naming: this guard has now cried wolf on 2 of 2 fully-successful deliveries. A verifier that fails closed is right, but one that fails closed on the happy path trains the human to ignore exactly the signal it exists to raise -- please add a regression case that runs the whole notify path against a real successful delivery and asserts OK, not just the failure modes.
